  Assignment 7: Functions of Several Variables, and Limits and Continuity.

  The first four problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for Functions of
  Several Variables" (pp-26, pp-27, rw17-6 and pp-25), split out into an
  assignment at the instructor's request, followed by the odd-numbered
  problems of the old worksheet "Exercises for Limits and Continuity in
  Higher Dimensions" (rm2-lim-1-a, rm2-lim-1-c, rm2-lim-3, rw23-2, rw21-6
  and rw22-6).  The first three problems of the old worksheet "Exercises
  for Partial Derivatives" (rm2-pd-1, pp-28 and q1-3) closed this
  assignment until the instructor had them moved to the start of
  assignment-8-chain-rule.ptx, in this student-facing skeletal book only;
  the other three books still have them here.  The remaining problems of
  all three worksheets went to review-problems-3.ptx.

  rw17-6 once had a second part asking for the normal line to the level
  surface; it was removed at the instructor's request before the split.

  The five quadric-surface problems at the start (exercise-quadric-graph-match,
  rf-5, rw23-7, rw21-10 and m2b-2) were moved here from
  assignment-6-quadric-surfaces.ptx at the instructor's request, in this
  student-facing skeletal book only: the copies in the other three books still
  have them in Assignment 6, so the books differ on purpose.  Their
  SOLUTION-LOCKED markers carry this assignment's number and due date.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-7" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 7</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems match quadric surfaces to their equations and identify and
sketch a quadric surface by slicing it with the coordinate planes, sketch
domains of functions of two variables, reason about level curves, identify
and sketch level surfaces, and ask when a limit of a function of two
variables exists and how to show that one does not. If you would like to
review the material first, see
<xref ref="sec-skel-geom-quadric-surfaces" text="title"/>,
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-functions" text="title"/> and
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-limits" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-quadric-graph-match">
  <title>Matching Quadric Surfaces</title>
  <introduction>
    <image xml:id="image-quadric-match-grid" width="100%">
      <shortdescription>
        Six quadric surfaces labeled A through F: an ellipsoid, a cone
        opening along the y axis, a saddle, a cylinder parallel to the
        y axis, a paraboloid opening along the positive y axis, and two
        bowl-shaped pieces meeting at the origin.
      </shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[\scalebox{0.62}{
\begin{tikzpicture}
\definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
\pgfplotsset{
  quadric/.style={
    view={115}{20},
    width=5.6cm,
    axis lines=center,
    axis on top,
    axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.5pt},
    xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
    label style={font=\normalsize},
    ticks=none,
    clip=false,
  },
  surfstyle/.style 2 args={
    surf, shader=faceted,
    colormap={soft#1}{color=(#2!14!white) color=(#2!48!white)},
    faceted color=black!45, line width=0.15pt,
    z buffer=sort,
  },
}
% ---- A: ellipsoid ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(0cm,0cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-1.9, ymax=1.9, zmin=-1.9, zmax=1.9]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={A}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:180, samples y=19]
    ({sin(y)*cos(x)}, {1.3*sin(y)*sin(x)}, {1.6*cos(y)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (2.8cm, -5.3cm) {A};
% ---- B: cone along y ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(6cm,0cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-2.3, xmax=2.3, ymin=-2.4, ymax=2.4, zmin=-1.9, zmax=1.9]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={B}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=-2:2, samples y=19]
    ({y*cos(x)}, {y}, {0.707*y*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (8.8cm, -5.3cm) {B};
% ---- C: saddle y = x^2 - z^2 ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(12cm,0cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.7, xmax=1.7, ymin=-2.4, ymax=2.4, zmin=-1.7, zmax=1.7]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={C}{oiblue},
      domain=-1.5:1.5, samples=21, y domain=-1.5:1.5, samples y=19]
    ({x}, {x^2 - y^2}, {y});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (14.8cm, -5.3cm) {C};
% ---- D: cylinder x^2 + 2z^2 = 1 ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(0cm,-6.4cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-2.4, ymax=2.4, zmin=-1.4, zmax=1.4]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={D}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=-2:2, samples y=15]
    ({cos(x)}, {y}, {0.707*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (2.8cm, -11.7cm) {D};
% ---- E: paraboloid y = 2x^2 + z^2 ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(6cm,-6.4cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.4, xmax=1.4, ymin=-0.5, ymax=3.0, zmin=-1.6, zmax=1.6]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={E}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:1.5, samples y=15]
    ({0.707*y*cos(x)}, {y*y}, {y*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (8.8cm, -11.7cm) {E};
% ---- F: two bowls y = +-(x^2 + z^2) ----
\begin{axis}[quadric, at={(12cm,-6.4cm)}, anchor=north west,
    xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-2.6, ymax=2.6, zmin=-1.6, zmax=1.6]
  \addplot3[surfstyle={F}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:1.5, samples y=15]
    ({y*cos(x)}, {y*y}, {y*sin(x)});
  \addplot3[surfstyle={F2}{oiblue},
      domain=0:360, samples=29, y domain=0:1.5, samples y=15]
    ({y*cos(x)}, {-y*y}, {y*sin(x)});
\end{axis}
\node[font=\Large\bfseries] at (14.8cm, -11.7cm) {F};
\end{tikzpicture}
}]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </introduction>
  <webwork>
    <pg-code>
      Context()->strings->add(
        "A" => {}, "B" => {}, "C" => {},
        "D" => {}, "E" => {}, "F" => {},
      );
      $match1 = String("E");
      $match2 = String("B");
      $match3 = String("D");
      $match4 = String("C");
    </pg-code>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Match the equations of the surfaces with the graphs A<ndash/>F shown
        above by entering a letter from A to F in each blank.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y = 2x^2 + z^2</m> matches graph <var name="$match1" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y^2 = x^2 + 2z^2</m> matches graph <var name="$match2" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = 1</m> matches graph <var name="$match3" width="4"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y = x^2 - z^2</m> matches graph <var name="$match4" width="4"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <!-- Also SOLUTION-LOCKED for assignment="7": WeBWorK shows this problem from
         its cached copies in generated-assets/webwork (exercise-quadric-graph-match.xml
         and webwork-representations.xml), whose four answers and solution carry the
         same markers. Release those two blocks with this one. -->
    <!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="7" due="2026-11-06"
    <solution>
      <p>
        We identify each surface by slicing, just as in this section.  Here
        the special axis is the <m>y</m>-axis, so we slice with the planes
        <m>y = c</m>, <m>x = c</m>, and <m>z = c</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>y = 2x^2 + z^2</m>, the plane <m>y = c</m> gives
        <m>2x^2 + z^2 = c</m>: no trace for <m>c \lt 0</m>, a single point
        when <m>c = 0</m>, and ellipses that grow as <m>c</m> increases.  The
        planes <m>x = c</m> and <m>z = c</m> give the parabolas
        <m>y = z^2 + 2c^2</m> and <m>y = 2x^2 + c^2</m>, both opening in the
        positive <m>y</m> direction.  Ellipses on one side only together with
        parabolas: an elliptical paraboloid opening along the positive
        <m>y</m>-axis, graph E.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>y^2 = x^2 + 2z^2</m>, the plane <m>y = c</m> gives
        <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = c^2</m>: an ellipse for every <m>c \neq 0</m>, on
        both sides of the origin, shrinking to a single point when
        <m>c = 0</m>.  The plane <m>z = 0</m> gives <m>y^2 = x^2</m>, the two
        crossing lines <m>y = \pm x</m>.  Ellipses collapsing to a point,
        together with crossing lines: an elliptic cone along the
        <m>y</m>-axis, graph B.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = 1</m>, the variable <m>y</m> is missing, so the
        plane <m>y = c</m> gives the <em>same</em> ellipse
        <m>x^2 + 2z^2 = 1</m> for every value of <m>c</m>.  Identical
        elliptical slices at every station: a cylinder parallel to the
        <m>y</m>-axis, graph D.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>y = x^2 - z^2</m>, the plane <m>z = c</m> gives the parabola
        <m>y = x^2 - c^2</m> opening in the positive <m>y</m> direction,
        while the plane <m>x = c</m> gives the parabola <m>y = c^2 - z^2</m>
        opening in the negative <m>y</m> direction, and the plane
        <m>y = 0</m> gives the crossing lines <m>z = \pm x</m>.  Parabolas
        opening in opposite directions: a hyperbolic paraboloid, a saddle,
        graph C.
      </p>
      <p>
        Graphs A and F match none of the equations.  Slicing the ellipsoid
        in A gives bounded ellipses in every direction, which none of the
        equations produce.  Slicing the two bowls in F with planes
        <m>y = c</m> gives ellipses whose size grows like
        <m>\sqrt{|c|}</m>, unlike the straight-sided linear growth of the
        cone in B.
      </p>
    </solution>
    END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
  </webwork>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-5">
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface corresponding to the following
equation:
<md>
z^2 - y^2 - x^2 = 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="7" due="2026-11-06"
<solution>
<p>
We can rewrite the equation as <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m>. Let us look at
its traces:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>z = c</m>: the horizontal traces <m>x^2 + y^2 = c^2</m> are circles
of radius <m>|c|</m> (for <m>c \ne 0</m>).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x = 0</m>: <m>z = \pm y</m>, a pair of lines intersecting at the
origin.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = 0</m>: <m>z = \pm x</m>, a pair of lines intersecting at the
origin.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
The surface is a <term>double cone</term> with its vertex at the
origin, opening along the <m>z</m>-axis. See
<xref ref="fig-rf-cone"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rf-cone">
<caption>
The surface <m>z^2 = x^2 + y^2</m>: a double cone with vertex at the
origin. Horizontal cross sections are circles of radius <m>|z|</m>.
</caption>
<image width="56%">
<description>
<p>
A double cone drawn in a three dimensional coordinate system: an
upper cone opening upward along the z axis and a mirror image cone
opening downward, meeting at a single vertex at the origin. Each cone
is capped by an ellipse representing a circular cross section.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-2.6) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,2.8) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-2.4,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (2.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.95,0.68) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (-1.3,-0.94) node[below left] {$x$};
  % upper cone
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (-1.5,1.8);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (1.5,1.8);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,1.8) ellipse (1.5cm and 0.34cm);
  % lower cone
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (-1.5,-1.8);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (1.5,-1.8);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (0,-1.8) ellipse (1.5cm and 0.34cm);
  % vertex
  \fill (0,0) circle (1.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-7">
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface <m>x^2 - y^2 + z^2 - 4 = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="7" due="2026-11-06"
<solution>
<p>
We can rewrite the equation as <m>x^2 + z^2 = y^2 + 4</m>. Let us look
at its traces:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = c</m>: <m>x^2 + z^2 = c^2 + 4</m>, circles with radius
<m>\sqrt{c^2+4} \ge 2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x = c</m>: <m>z^2 - y^2 = 4 - c^2</m>, hyperbolas.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>z = c</m>: <m>x^2 - y^2 = 4 - c^2</m>, hyperbolas (for
<m>c = 0</m>: <m>x^2 - y^2 = 4</m>).
</p>
</li>
</ul>
The surface is a <term>hyperboloid of one sheet</term> whose axis is
the <m>y</m>-axis. See <xref ref="fig-rw23-hyperboloid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw23-hyperboloid">
<caption>
The hyperboloid of one sheet <m>x^2 + z^2 = y^2 + 4</m>, opening along
the <m>y</m>-axis. The narrowest circle, of radius 2, occurs at
<m>y = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A hyperboloid of one sheet drawn with its axis along the horizontal y
axis: a surface shaped like a cooling tower lying on its side, with a
narrow circular waist at the center and wide circular openings at the
left and right ends, outlined by two large end ellipses and hyperbola
shaped side curves.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-2.4) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-3.5,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (3.7,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.9,0.65) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (-1.25,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
  % end ellipses at y = +-3, radius sqrt(13) ~ 2.1 visually
  \draw[mred, thick] (-3,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 2.1cm);
  \draw[mred, thick] (3,0) ellipse (0.42cm and 2.1cm);
  % waist ellipse at y = 0, radius 2 -> 1.15 visually
  \draw[mred, thick] (0,0) ellipse (0.24cm and 1.15cm);
  % hyperbola sides: top and bottom profiles z = +-sqrt(y^2/ (9/ (13-4)) ... use plot
  \draw[mred, thick] plot[domain=-3:3, samples=41]
    (\x, {0.60*sqrt(\x*\x + 3.67)});
  \draw[mred, thick] plot[domain=-3:3, samples=41]
    (\x, {-0.60*sqrt(\x*\x + 3.67)});
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-10">
<statement>
<p>
Identify and sketch the surface corresponding to the equation
<m>2x^2 - y + z^2 = 0</m>. Give reasons for your choice.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="7" due="2026-11-06"
<solution>
<p>
We can rewrite the equation as <m>y = 2x^2 + z^2</m>. Let us look at
its traces:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = 0</m>: <m>2x^2 + z^2 = 0</m> forces <m>x = z = 0</m>, the
single point <m>(0,0,0)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>x = 0</m>: <m>y = z^2</m>, a parabola in the <m>yz</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>z = 0</m>: <m>y = 2x^2</m>, a parabola in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>y = c \gt 0</m>: <m>2x^2 + z^2 = c</m>, ellipses.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
The surface is an <term>elliptic paraboloid</term> with vertex at the
origin, opening along the positive <m>y</m>-axis. See
<xref ref="fig-rw21-paraboloid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw21-paraboloid">
<caption>
The elliptic paraboloid <m>y = 2x^2 + z^2</m>: a bowl with its vertex
at the origin, opening along the positive <m>y</m>-axis. Cross
sections <m>y = c \gt 0</m> are ellipses.
</caption>
<image width="58%">
<description>
<p>
A bowl shaped surface in a three dimensional coordinate system with
its vertex at the origin, opening to the right along the positive y
axis. The bowl is outlined by two parabola shaped profile curves and
capped by an ellipse at its wide right end.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  % axes
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0,-2.3) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (0,2.5) node[above] {$z$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (-1.6,0) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (4.1,0) node[right] {$y$};
  \draw[-stealth, thick] (0.9,0.65) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (-1.25,-0.9) node[below left] {$x$};
  % paraboloid profile: z = +-0.95*sqrt(y), vertex at origin, opening +y
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=0:3.4, samples=31]
    (\x, {0.95*sqrt(\x)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] plot[domain=0:3.4, samples=31]
    (\x, {-0.95*sqrt(\x)});
  \draw[mblue, thick] (3.4,0) ellipse (0.32cm and 1.75cm);
  % vertex
  \fill (0,0) circle (1.6pt) node[below left=1pt] {$(0,0,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-2">
<statement>
<p>
Sketch the surface <m>4x^2 - y^2 = -4 - z^2</m> in three dimensions.
Label any points where the surface crosses the coordinate axes.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="7" due="2026-11-06"
<solution>
<p>
Collect everything on the left and divide by <m>-4</m>:
<md>
<mrow>4x^2 - y^2 + z^2 \amp = -4</mrow>
<mrow>-x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4} - \frac{z^2}{4} \amp = 1</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{y^2}{4} - x^2 - \frac{z^2}{4} \amp = 1 .</mrow>
</md>
Two minus signs and one plus sign, with the constant <m>1</m> on the
right, identify this as a <term>hyperboloid of two sheets</term>. The
axis is the one belonging to the positive term, so the axis is the
<m>y</m>-axis.
</p>

<p>
The traces confirm this.
<ul>
<li>
<p>
Setting <m>y = k</m> gives <m>4x^2 + z^2 = k^2-4</m>, which is an
ellipse when <m>|k| \gt 2</m>, a single point when <m>|k| = 2</m>, and
empty when <m>|k| \lt 2</m>. So there is nothing at all in the slab
<m>-2 \lt y \lt 2</m>: this is why there are two separate sheets.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Setting <m>z = 0</m> gives <m>y^2/4 - x^2 = 1</m>, a hyperbola opening
along the <m>y</m>-axis, and setting <m>x = 0</m> gives
<m>y^2/4 - z^2/4 = 1</m>, likewise.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
<term>Axis crossings.</term> Setting <m>y = z = 0</m> gives
<m>4x^2 = -4</m>, impossible; setting <m>x = y = 0</m> gives
<m>z^2 = -4</m>, impossible. Setting <m>x = z = 0</m> gives
<m>y^2 = 4</m>, so the surface meets the axes only at the two vertices
<md>
(0,\ 2,\ 0) \qquad\text{and}\qquad (0,\ -2,\ 0) .
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-m2b-hyperboloid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-hyperboloid">
<caption>
The hyperboloid of two sheets
<m>\tfrac{y^2}{4} - x^2 - \tfrac{z^2}{4} = 1</m>, with vertices at
<m>(0,\pm2,0)</m>. Nothing at all lies in the slab
<m>-2 \lt y \lt 2</m>, which is why the two sheets are separate.
</caption>
<image width="74%">
<description>
<p>
Two bowl shaped sheets opening away from each other along the y axis,
with vertices at y equals two and y equals negative two. The region
between the two vertices is empty.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
  width=12cm, height=9cm,
  view={-72}{16},
  xmin=-2.4, xmax=2.4, ymin=-5.4, ymax=5.4, zmin=-4.4, zmax=4.4,
  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
  xtick={-2,0,2}, ytick={-4,-2,0,2,4}, ztick={-4,0,4},
  clip=false,
]
% right-hand sheet: x = sinh(u)cos(v), y = 2cosh(u), z = 2 sinh(u) sin(v)
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=mblue, draw=mblue,
          fill opacity=0.30, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:1.45, y domain=0:360, samples=11, samples y=37,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sinh(x)*cos(y)},{2*cosh(x)},{2*sinh(x)*sin(y)});
% left-hand sheet
\addplot3[surf, shader=flat, fill=mgreen, draw=mgreen,
          fill opacity=0.30, draw opacity=0.35,
          domain=0:1.45, y domain=0:360, samples=11, samples y=37,
          z buffer=sort]
  ({sinh(x)*cos(y)},{-2*cosh(x)},{2*sinh(x)*sin(y)});
% vertices
\addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2pt, mred]
  coordinates {(0,2,0) (0,-2,0)};
\node[mred, anchor=north east, inner sep=2pt, fill=white,
      fill opacity=0.75, text opacity=1] at (axis cs:0,2,0) {$(0,2,0)$};
\node[mred, anchor=north west, inner sep=2pt, fill=white,
      fill opacity=0.75, text opacity=1] at (axis cs:0,-2,0) {$(0,-2,0)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
